      "text": "To find the sum of the greatest common factor (GCF) of 3 and 6 and the least common multiple (LCM) of 3 and 6, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the GCF of 3 and 6.\n   - The factors of 3 are 1 and 3.\n   - The factors of 6 are 1, 2, 3, and 6.\n   - The common factors of 3 and 6 are 1 and 3.\n   - The greatest common factor (GCF) is the largest of these common factors, which is 3.\n   \n   So, the GCF of 3 and 6 is 3.\n\n2. Identify the LCM of 3 and 6.\n   - The multiples of 3 are 3, 6, 9, 12, ...\n   - The multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18, ...\n   - The least common multiple (LCM) is the smallest of these multiples, which is 6.\n   \n   So, the LCM of 3 and 6 is 6.\n\n3. Calculate the sum of the GCF and the LCM.\n   - The GCF is 3.\n   - The LCM is 6.\n   - The sum is \\(3 + 6 = 9\\).\n\nTherefore, the sum of the greatest common factor of 3 and 6 and the least common multiple of 3 and 6 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
